  "query_preview": "..\nCurrently there is lingering clouds with a few showers along\nthe AZ/NM border and south of the international border, with\nsome of the stronger storms having a lightning strike or two.\nThe latest CAMs have little to isolated activity the rest of\ntonight. \n\nLooking into today, the moisture is still in place with a west \nto east gradient of PWs of 1.0 to 1.7 inches with the highest \nvalues Tucson eastward. The upper level pattern has a low\npressure system to the NW and Hurricane Marie to the SW of the\narea and high pressure to the west keeps the South to Southwest\nflow aloft.  This is area is the CAMs are highlighting for \nactivity this afternoon and evening. This lines up with the \nSlight Risk of flash flooding focusing in on areas east of \nTucson. Similar to yesterday, storms will be efficient rainers \nwith accumulations of isolated 1 to 2 inches of rainfall \npossible with any storm that could lead to flash flooding. Areas\nthat had rainfall today will see quicker responses in creeks, \nrivers, and washes, especially in Graham, Greenlee, and Cochise \ncounties. \n\nAs the low pressure system moves to cut off from the main flow\nand Hurricane Marie moves west, moisture continues to move in,\nwith the bulk of the moisture to the north of the area, however\nexpecting similar PWs similar in current values continue into \nearly next week. This keeps broad slight chances (30-50%) for\nareas south and east of Tucson.\n\nTemperatures will be hovering near normal to slightly below\nnormal through the forecast period.",
